  { q: 'How do I know which level I am?', a: 'Go by how long you have trained consistently, not by how much you lift. The American College of Sports Medicine defines a novice as someone with no resistance training experience or who has not trained for several years, an intermediate as someone with about 6 months of consistent training, and an advanced lifter as someone with years of experience. Jordan\'s definition of a true beginner is stricter: someone who has never played a sport, done anything physical, or formally lifted. Gym class in middle or high school does not count.' },
  { q: 'How many days a week should I train?', a: 'The ACSM recommends 2 to 3 training days a week for novices, 3 to 4 for intermediates and 4 to 5 for advanced lifters. Jordan says that for most people 3 to 5 days a week is enough, and that frequency is limited by how quickly you can recover.' },
  { q: 'Should I follow a program or make it up as I go?', a: 'Follow a program that fits you. Jordan says most people do not really pick a program. They follow random advice from social media or people with good physiques. He says to look at who wrote it, how much experience that person has and whether it fits your goals, and he is blunt that giving everyone the same program with nothing individualized is "cheap" and "dishonest".' },
  { q: 'When do I move up a level?', a: 'When your training age catches up, not when you hit a number. Early on, most of your progress is neurological, and gains come fast and then slow. Jordan warns beginners not to assume they will keep advancing at that early rate, and says not to progress yourself too fast.' },
