  <h2>The three levels</h2>
  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <table class="pct-table data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Who (ACSM)</th><th>Loads</th><th>Training days a week</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Novice</td><td class="wrap">No resistance training experience, or has not trained for several years</td><td class="wrap">8 to 12 rep max</td><td>2 to 3</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Intermediate</td><td class="wrap">About 6 months of consistent training</td><td class="wrap">1 to 12 rep max, periodized, eventually emphasizing heavy 1 to 6</td><td>3 to 4</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Advanced</td><td class="wrap">Years of training experience</td><td class="wrap">1 to 12 rep max, periodized, eventually emphasizing heavy 1 to 6 (the ACSM groups intermediate and advanced loading together)</td><td>4 to 5</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="chart-note">From the ACSM position stand on progression models in resistance training for healthy adults (2009). These are guidelines for healthy adults, not rules, and training days are for your whole week, not only the bench.</p>
  <p>To see how your actual numbers compare, use the <a href="/bench-press-standards/">bench press standards</a>. They show where lifters land by bodyweight, age and sex.</p>

  <h2>Beginner</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Jordan's definition.</strong> A true beginner has never played a sport, done anything physical with their body, or formally lifted weights.</li>
    <li><strong>The first weeks.</strong> He starts with an exploratory week: about 5 to 15 exercises or machines, 1 to 3 sets each, to see how the person moves, what they like and how their form looks. He teaches movement patterns more than specific lifts.</li>
    <li><strong>What to learn first.</strong> The fundamentals, and perfecting them before anything more advanced. Beginners get rapid early gains, get excited, and assume the pace will hold. It will not.</li>
    <li><strong>Adding weight.</strong> Add 5 pounds, at most 10 per set, at a pace that depends on the program, your progress and your goals.</li>
    <li><strong>If you stall.</strong> It is usually form: not positioned correctly, not using the right muscles in the right order, or movements that are not smooth and coordinated.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Research agrees that beginners gain fastest. In a pooled analysis of periodized and non-periodized programs (Williams and colleagues, 2017), strength gains were larger in untrained participants.</p>
  <p><strong>Example structure (not a prescription).</strong> Two or three bench days a week, each with 3 sets of 8 to 10 reps at a weight that leaves a couple of reps in the tank, adding a small amount of weight when you hit every rep.</p>

  <h2>Intermediate</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Periodize.</strong> The ACSM recommends moving through a wider rep range over time, ending with heavier 1 to 6 rep work, with 3 to 5 minutes of rest between heavy sets and a moderate rep speed of about 1 to 2 seconds up and 1 to 2 seconds down.</li>
    <li><strong>Vary the stimulus.</strong> Moesgaard and colleagues (2022) found undulating periodization, with weekly or daily changes in volume and intensity, beat linear periodization for maximal strength in trained lifters. The estimate was moderate to large (0.61) but uncertain.</li>
    <li><strong>A progression rule.</strong> When you can do one or two reps more than your target at a given load, the ACSM says to increase the load by 2 to 10 percent.</li>
    <li><strong>Jordan's order of changes.</strong> Add reps first, then weight. Do not use the same load forever.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Example structure.</strong> Three bench days: a heavy day, a volume day and a technique day, as in the <a href="/rpe-bench-press-program/">RPE bench press program</a>.</p>

  <h2>Advanced</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>More days, more planning.</strong> The ACSM recommends 4 to 5 training days a week with periodized heavy work.</li>
    <li><strong>What strong athletes do on average.</strong> In a meta-analysis of collegiate and professional athletes (Peterson and colleagues, 2004), maximal strength gains were greatest at a mean intensity of 85 percent of one rep max, 2 days a week, and about 8 sets per muscle group. That describes athletes in studies, so it is context and not a template.</li>
    <li><strong>Break plateaus with tempo.</strong> Jordan's first moves when adding weight stops working are a slower lowering phase or a 3 second pause at the bottom. See <a href="/controlling-eccentric-on-bench-press-tips/">tempo and eccentric control</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Finish a block properly.</strong> Toward the end, mix in faster dynamic lifts, then move light weight very fast and maximal weight for very low reps.</li>
    <li><strong>Test sensibly.</strong> Jordan uses a 3 or 5 rep max as a stand-in for a one rep max, and says numbers from higher rep sets are not very reliable.</li>
    <li><strong>Deload.</strong> Cut volume in half and keep the weights about the same. See <a href="/how-to-deload-bench-press-properly/">how to deload your bench</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Moving up a level</h2>
  <p>Frequency goes up with experience, but recovery sets the limit. Jordan says frequency is based on how quickly you can recover, and that it cannot outpace recovery if you want continued quality progress. See <a href="/bench-press-programming/frequency/">how often to bench press</a>. He also advises against progressing too fast and against making promises to yourself that you cannot keep.</p>

  <h2>What this evidence cannot tell you</h2>
  <p>The ACSM categories are broad guidelines for healthy adults, and much of the research behind them mixes lifters of different levels. The meta-analyses cited here cover many exercises, not only the bench press. Use the structures above as starting points, change one thing at a time, and judge each change over 8 to 12 weeks.</p>
